  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe initial cold front has stalled and become stationary near \nthe New England Coast and south of the region this morning with \nlingering low clouds and some patchy fog across the region. Some\nclearing north of the Capital Region towards Lake George may \nallow for some localized areas of fog to redevelop. Otherwise, \nabundant low-level moisture in place will allow for low stratus \nto continue into the late morning/early pm, as a secondary cold\nfront moves through the region in the late morning into the \nearly afternoon. Temps today will run below normal by 5-10 \ndegrees with mid 50s to mid/upper 60s for highs with N/NE winds \n5-15 mph and some gusts 20-25 mph. High pressure will be \nbuilding south tonight from southeast Canada. A canopy of mid to\nhigh clouds associated with low pressure moving along the mid- \nAtlantic Region and its associated inverted sfc trough will \noverspread the region late this afternoon into tonight. A few \nisolated light showers (15-24% chance)/sprinkles may get close \nto the I-84 corridor tonight. Clearing over the northern zones \nmay allow lows to fall into mid 30s to around 40F. Some patchy \nfrost is possible over the southwest Adirondacks and portions of\nsouthern VT. Lows will be cool in the 40s from I-90 and the \nCapital Region southward. \n\nA strong surface anticyclone will ridge in over the region from\nthe northern Great Lakes Region, southeast Ontario and Quebec \nfrom Tue through the mid week. Temps will continue to run below \nnormal on Tue with mid 50s to around 60F over the higher terrain\nand lower to mid 60s in the valleys with partly sunny \nconditions especially from the Capital Region north. Autumn \nbegins at 805 pm Tuesday. A chilly night is expected with near \nideal radiational cooling conditions north of the mid Hudson \nValley Tue night/Wed morning. Some areas of frost are possible \nover the southwest Adirondacks with lows 33-36F. Patchy frost is\npossible over the southern Greens. Lows in the 30s will be \ncommon north and east of the Capital District/Mohawk Valley, \nexcept some upper 30s over the northern Catskills. Expect lower \nto mid 40s over the rest of the region. Temps rebound closer to\nnormal Wed and Thu with dry conditions continuing with the \nstrong surface high over s-cntral Quebec, as a mid and upper \nlevel Rex Block sets-up. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe late week into the weekend forecast period, the ensembles, \ndeterministic medium range and AI guidance continue to show a \npotential coastal low forming and tracking near the Mid Atlantic\nand New England Coasts. The various solutions continue to vary \non the placement and track. With the Rex Block setting up aloft \nwhich is common this time of year, the coastal low position is \nuncertain where it will close off. The latest Days 4-7 WPC \nguidance does bring a chance of showers (30-40%) from the \nnorthern Catskills, Greater Capital Region, southern VT south \nand east with the coastal low well south and east of Long Island\nFri pm-Fri night. Chances of showers continue for at least the \nthe opening of the weekend from the Capital Region south and \neast. If the storm tracks close enough to southeast New England \nand Long Island, then it will also become breezy with \nnorth/northeast winds late Fri into the weekend. The amount of \nrainfall is uncertain with the variability on the track of the \nlow going into the weekend. Block flow regimes and closed lows \nare challenging to pinpoint the timing, evolution and pcpn \ndistributions far out in time. We will continue to monitor this\nsystem and additional changes to the forecast late-week should \nbe expected.",
